# The agent recording for the site's drawer slide, played by vhs (https://github.com/charmbracelet/vhs):
#
#   cargo build --release && vhs docs/agent.tape
#
# Then shrink it exactly as docs/demo.tape's is shrunk — same reasoning, same two passes:
#
#   ffmpeg -y -i docs/agent.gif -vf palettegen=max_colors=64:stats_mode=diff /tmp/p.png
#   ffmpeg -y -i docs/agent.gif -i /tmp/p.png -lavfi paletteuse=dither=none /tmp/small.gif
#   mv /tmp/small.gif docs/agent.gif
#
# The third line is not decoration. ffmpeg 9 refuses an output file it is also reading —
# "Output docs/agent.gif same as Input #0" — where older ones let the in-place form through, so
# the second pass writes somewhere else and the file is moved back. 0.79 MB becomes 0.60 MB,
# and 64 colours is still plenty: the four marks in the launcher are the only real pixel art in
# the recording and they come through it unharmed.
#
# Read docs/demo.tape's header first: the sizing (1800x1000 is 175x46 cells), the font's own
# family name, why `dither=none` is the load-bearing half of the shrink, and why everything goes
# through plain Ctrl chords and the command palette rather than the chords a real user presses.
# All of it holds here. What follows is only what this tape adds, and the first item is large.
#
# ---- This one is not deterministic ------------------------------------------------------------
#
# docs/demo.tape drives a program that answers the same way every time. This one starts a real
# `claude` under the recorder's own account and asks it a question, and the answer is written
# fresh on every take: different wording, different pauses, sometimes a different number of tool
# calls. So this GIF is re-recorded until a take is good — the way the pictures in
# docs/screenshots are re-shot by hand — rather than regenerated byte for byte. The sleeps below
# are the ones that held for the take that shipped; a slower day needs longer ones, and the
# honest way to find out is to record it and look at the frames.
#
# The fixture is a throwaway directory made off camera, with one small Python file in it. The
# agent is pointed at that and never at this repository: a tape that lets a model loose in the
# checkout it is recording would be a demo of something nobody should copy.
#
# ---- Three things are answered off camera, and why -------------------------------------------
#
# 1. The folder's trust dialog. Claude Code asks, once per directory, whether you trust what is
#    in it — and this directory was made forty seconds ago by the tape itself. It is a question
#    about a folder, not about the edit, and answering it on camera would be a wall of text in a
#    drawer that has nothing to do with CleeCode. So the tape starts `claude` once in the fixture
#    while hidden, answers it, and leaves. The cost is one entry per take in the recorder's own
#    ~/.claude.json, naming a temporary directory that no longer exists; `claude project purge
#    <path>` removes one if the clutter bothers you.
# 2. The edits. The fixture carries a `.claude/settings.json` that pre-approves Edit, Write and
#    Read, so the agent does not stop to ask halfway through the shot. This is what a person who
#    trusts a scratch directory would have set anyway.
# 3. The status line, and the suggested next prompt. The same file turns both off. The author's
#    status line prints his usage and what the month has cost him, and neither of those belongs
#    in a picture on a website; the suggestion is grey text that appears in the input box on its
#    own, and in a recording it reads as somebody typing rather than as an offer.
#
# ---- Getting the keyboard back out of the drawer ----------------------------------------------
#
# A focused agent has first claim on Ctrl, for the same reason a focused shell does — it needs
# its own Ctrl+C — so once claude is running, Ctrl+P is claude's and the palette is out of reach.
# The way out is Ctrl+Alt+Left, which moves the focus one frame to the left and is the one route
# vhs can actually send: Ctrl+Tab and every Ctrl+Shift chord are beyond it, as demo.tape explains.

Output docs/agent.gif

Set Shell "bash"
Set FontFamily "JetBrainsMono NFM"
Set FontSize 16
Set Width 1800
Set Height 1000
Set Padding 12
Set Framerate 20

# Off camera. Same throwaway config and same `clee` on PATH as demo.tape, then the fixture.
Hide
# Recorded from inside a Claude Code session, this shell carries that session's markers, and the
# agent started below would inherit them and print a warning about transcripts across the top of
# the drawer. Unset on a machine that has none of them, this line does nothing at all.
Type `unset $(env | sed -n 's/^\(CLAUDE[A-Z_]*\)=.*/\1/p')`
Enter
Type "export XDG_CONFIG_HOME=$(mktemp -d) BIN=$(mktemp -d)"
Enter
Type "ln -sf $PWD/target/release/clee $BIN/clee && export PATH=$BIN:$PATH"
Enter

# The fixture: two functions and two Italian comments, which is exactly one small job to ask for.
Type "export PROJ=$(mktemp -d)/cerchio && mkdir -p $PROJ/.claude && cd $PROJ"
Enter
Type "cat > main.py <<'PY'"
Enter
Type "PI = 3.14159"
Enter
Enter
Type "def area(raggio):"
Enter
Type "    # Calcola l'area del cerchio a partire dal raggio"
Enter
Type "    return PI * raggio * raggio"
Enter
Enter
Type "def perimetro(raggio):"
Enter
Type "    # Restituisce la circonferenza dello stesso cerchio"
Enter
Type "    return 2 * PI * raggio"
Enter
Enter
Type "print(area(2), perimetro(2))"
Enter
Type "PY"
Enter
Type `printf '%s' '{"permissions": {"defaultMode": "acceptEdits", "allow": ["Edit", "Write", "Read"]}, "statusLine": {"type": "command", "command": "true"}, "promptSuggestionEnabled": false}' > .claude/settings.json`
Enter

# The trust dialog, answered where nobody has to watch it. Enter takes the highlighted first
# answer, which is the one that says yes.
Type "claude"
Enter
Sleep 14s
Enter
Sleep 8s
Type "/exit"
Enter
Sleep 4s
Type "clear"
Enter
Sleep 1s
Show

# `clee .` with the path spelled out: no splash. Deliberate — the splash is docs/demo.tape's
# opening shot and this tape has sixty seconds to spend on something else.
Type "clee ."
Enter
Sleep 4s

# ---- The file, open, so there is a buffer for the edit to land in ------------------ ~6s
Ctrl+o
Sleep 1s
Type "main.py"
Sleep 1.2s
Enter
Sleep 3s

# ---- The drawer, on its launcher: four agents, each drawn as its own mark ---------- ~7s
#      The highlight is walked one step down and back so it reads as a list you choose from
#      rather than a picture of one. It lands on claude, which is where it started: a fresh
#      config remembers nobody, and the order is the order they are declared in.
Ctrl+p
Sleep 1s
Type "Agent drawer"
Sleep 1.2s
Enter
Sleep 2.5s
Down
Sleep 1.2s
Up
Sleep 2s

# ---- claude, started for real, with `clee --mcp` already registered ---------------- ~14s
Enter
Sleep 14s

# ---- One small job, asked in one sentence ----------------------------------------- ~8s
#      "in a single edit" is not fussiness. Asked without it the model writes the two comments as
#      two Edit calls, which is two writes to the file, which is two reloads — and the second
#      one's lit lines are the only ones left, because each reload lights what *that* read
#      brought in. One edit, one reload, both lines lit: the picture the shot is for.
Type "Translate both comments in main.py to English, in a single edit. Change nothing else."
Sleep 1.5s
Enter
Sleep 6s

# ---- Back to the buffer, to watch the edit arrive in it ---------------------------- ~18s
#      This is the shot the whole tape exists for: nobody saves, nobody reloads, nobody switches
#      window. The file changes on disk under a clean buffer, CleeCode picks it up, and the lines
#      that arrived are lit in the gutter and behind the text until you type over them.
#
#      Twelve seconds is what the model took on the take that shipped, and it is the number most
#      likely to need moving: too few and the tape ends before the edit, too many and the GIF
#      spends its last quarter on a still picture. Split in two so the second half can grow
#      without the first being retyped.
Ctrl+Alt+Left
Sleep 12s
Sleep 6s

# End on the landed edit: the GIF loops, and this is the frame worth coming back to.
Sleep 4s
